{"kind":"assert","text":"Codex's changes-requested verdict 64109681 on head 08552c03 is correct, and the security finding is a false allow in code I wrote. Reporting the projection limitation under 3e9bac91, which is the path you pre-authorized for exactly this.\n\nTHE DEFECT, CONFIRMED IN MY OWN SOURCE. worldMovedAfter continues past a retired basis instead of traversing through it, so a basis's own bases are never walked once that basis is itself retired. Codex's chain is candidate to near to deep, with deep retired BEFORE the verdict and near retired AFTER it. The fold makes the candidate world-stale from deep, so it already described a superseded world when the reviewer signed. My walk sees only near, dates it after the verdict, and admits the merge. That is a false allow on a gate authorizing an irreversible merge and a cross-author retirement plan, which makes it an authority defect, not a nuisance. Codex reproduced it by name as TestReviewWorldMovedAfterDoesNotHideOlderNestedRetirement. I read my own code at the branch: the offending line is a bare continue where the basis should be enqueued.\n\nTHE HALF I CAN CLOSE. Traversing through retired bases instead of stopping finds every cause on the artifact-provenance closure, and any cause at or before the verdict then refuses. That closes the nested case.\n\nTHE HALF I CANNOT CLOSE FROM THE PROJECTION, WHICH IS THE ANSWER TO YOUR QUESTION. You asked me to say if the projection lacked what was needed. It exposes sequences -- Decisions carries one for every record including acts, which is what I reported last time and still stands. What it does not expose is which retirement causes remain ACTIVE. When a supersession is itself superseded, Projection.Acts still lists the original as an effective supersede statement, and nothing in the projection says whether its target is still retired. cmd/gs cannot tell a live cause from a withdrawn one. Counting withdrawn causes is fail-closed, so it produces false refusals rather than false allows, but it means restored histories cannot be dated faithfully and the guard would refuse merges the fold considers clean.\n\nSO THE HONEST POSITION IS NARROWER THAN MY LAST REPORT. I told you no widening was needed. That was right about sequence and wrong about sufficiency, and I did not notice the difference until codex showed me a chain where it matters.\n\nWHAT I THINK, AND WHY IT IS YOUR CALL. Codex's simplification conclusion is that one authoritative dated active-cause fact from the fold beats a parallel inference in cmd/gs that has already drifted, and I agree -- my walk is a second copy of the fold's staleness rule and it drifted on its first outing. But that is a projection-contract change: a new dated field, docs/reference/architecture.md updated in the same head, and a ProfileVersion bump, which is exactly the widening you scoped out. Three options, and I will take whichever you rule: authorize the fold-side dated active cause; or have me land the traversal fix alone as a deliberate fail-closed approximation, documented as refusing some merges the fold would allow; or close the lane and leave the post-verdict refusal in place.\n\nONE MORE CORRECTION OF MY OWN. The documentation I added at docs/reference/gs/merge.md says the merge dates each retirement it finds. Codex is right that this overstates the implementation, because the walk stops at the nearest retired basis. Whichever option you choose, that sentence is wrong as written and I will fix it in the same head.\n\nHead 08552c03 must not merge. The changes-requested verdict already blocks it, and I am not publishing a successor until you rule."}